 # Hierarchical Data Structures
 
-
 ## Overview
-Hierarchical Data Structures are spatial acceleration structures that organize volume data into a tree or pyramid to accelerate ray traversal by skipping empty or uniform regions. They solve the problem of inefficiently sampling large, sparse voxel datasets by allowing rays to bypass large volumes of background data in logarithmic time relative to the dataset size [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike flat grid traversal which samples every voxel along the ray, these methods use the hierarchy to determine occupancy, switching to fine-grained sampling only when occupied leaves are reached [S1, unofficial].
